{#
    The browser column of ADR 009 rule 11, middle row: authenticated, and refused.

    FRAMEWORK PLUMBING, NOT A PAGE. Symfony's `TwigErrorRenderer` looks up
    `@Twig/Exception/error{status}.html.twig` and this path overrides the bundle's own copy. There
    is no controller, no route and no card behind it: it is the shape of a refusal a person reads,
    and it is here rather than in a card because every guarded operation on the board shares it.

    IT SAYS NOTHING, AND THAT IS ADR 008 RULE 14. No permission name, no identifier, no hint about
    what exists. The JSON representation of this same refusal carries `shared.forbidden`, "You are
    not allowed to do that.", with no parameters; this page is that sentence with a way out under
    it, and the two must not drift into telling a reader different things. The permission that was
    refused goes to the log, next to the actor and the operation, which is where somebody debugging
    can use it.

    NO CATALOGUE ENTRY. ADR 007 governs the words a *failure* carries, and a failure carries a
    declared code that `FailureMessages` renders; page chrome is the delivery's own English, exactly
    as `security/login.html.twig`'s labels are. `bin/console app:messages` counts what it counted
    before this file.

    `status_text` is deliberately unused. Symfony passes it, it is RFC 9110's reason phrase, and it
    is protocol vocabulary rather than something to show a reader.
#}
{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}Not allowed{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
    <main class="page">
        <div class="stack-6" style="max-width:420px;margin:0 auto">
            <h1 class="display-md">Not allowed</h1>

            {% include 'components/_notice.html.twig' with {
                sentence: 'You are not allowed to do that.',
                tone: 'danger',
            } only %}

            <a class="btn" href="{{ path('app_login') }}">Sign in as somebody else</a>
        </div>
    </main>
{% endblock %}
